<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Norwegian Bokmål translation.

    A translation of `values/strings.xml`, which is the source (SPEC §9). It
    serves the Norwegian network of the catalogue and, like every other
    language here, any other city the reader installs. Where Android's own
    Norwegian has a word, that word is used; the arbiter is the phone itself.

    This is Bokmål, not Nynorsk.

    Rules, the same as everywhere: positional placeholders, <plurals> for
    anything that agrees, and the `name` attributes left untouched. Norwegian
    Bokmål distinguishes two plural categories, `one` and `other`.

    The typography is Norwegian: quotation marks are « », with no space inside
    them, and the dash that breaks a sentence is – with a space on either side
    rather than the em dash the English file uses. An ellipsis carries a space
    before it, which is what Android's own Norwegian prints — "Laster inn …"
    (`android:loading`). Nothing needs escaping: no straight apostrophe is
    written anywhere in the file. Compounds are written as one word, which is
    what lengthens the labels; where a button could not carry the literal
    translation it takes the shorter wording Android itself uses. The reader is
    addressed as `du`, and the impersonal turns of the English are kept
    impersonal.
    See docs/glossary/nb.md.
-->
